    id: 1, name: "Ada Lovelace", role: "Mathematician & Writer", company: "Analytical Engine", era: "The Foundation", nationality: "British", born: "December 10, 1815", died: "November 27, 1852",
    bio: "Ada Lovelace was born Augusta Ada Byron in London, the only legitimate child of the famous poet Lord Byron and his wife Anne Isabella Milbanke. Her parents separated just weeks after her birth, and Ada never knew her father, who left England permanently. Her mother, determined that Ada would not inherit her father's volatile poetic temperament, steered her aggressively toward mathematics and logic.\n\nGrowing up in a wealthy aristocratic household, Ada had access to the finest tutors. She studied under Mary Somerville, a renowned mathematician and astronomer, who became her mentor and friend. At age 17, Ada met Charles Babbage at a party. He showed her his 'Difference Engine,' a mechanical calculator. Ada was captivated. She understood its potential immediately, seeing beyond what even Babbage had imagined.\n\nIn 1842, Ada was asked to translate an article by Italian mathematician Luigi Menabrea about Babbage's newer invention, the Analytical Engine. Ada did not merely translate, she added extensive notes of her own that were three times longer than the original article. In 'Note G,' she described an algorithm for the Analytical Engine to compute Bernoulli numbers. This is now recognized as the first algorithm intended to be processed by a machine, making Ada the world's first computer programmer.\n\nAda saw what others could not: that the machine could manipulate symbols according to rules, and that numbers could represent not just quantities, but music, art, and virtually anything. She wrote that the engine 'might act upon other things besides number, were objects found whose mutual fundamental relations could be expressed by those of the abstract science of operations.' This leap of imagination, from calculation to general-purpose computation, was a century ahead of its time.\n\nAda died of uterine cancer at age 36, the same age her father had died. Her work was largely forgotten for a century until it was republished in 1953. The US Department of Defense named their programming language 'Ada' in her honor in 1980. Today, Ada Lovelace Day celebrates women in STEM worldwide, a fitting tribute to a woman who, in the 1840s, imagined the entire future of computing.",
    quotes: ["The Analytical Engine weaves algebraic patterns just as the Jacquard loom weaves flowers and leaves.", "Imagination is the Discovering Faculty, pre-eminently. It is that which penetrates into the unseen worlds around us."],
